    "body": "WARSAW, Poland — Estonia plans to close its road border crossings with Russia in response to an attempted arson attack against Estonian defense company Threod Systems that took place on Saturday, the country’s officials announced.\nIn the early hours of Nov. 14, Estonian law enforcement detained eight Latvian citizens suspected of attempting to set fire to the company’s facility in Viimsi, near the country’s capital, Tallinn, and cause an explosion.\n“Based on an assessment of information from the Internal Security Service, this attack was carried out at the behest of the GRU, Russia’s military intelligence service,” Margo Palloson, the director general of the Estonian Internal Security Service (KAPO), was quoted in a statement released by the nation’s Prosecutor’s Office.\nThe facility did not sustain significant damage, as Threod Systems’ security personnel thwarted the attack in its initial phase, the office said.\nCompany spokeswoman Maarja Karin told Defense News there was no disruption in production activities.\n“We are investigating the case under the provision of the Estonian Penal Code concerning activities directed against the security of the Republic of Estonia carried out on behalf of a foreign intelligence or security service, including gathering information or damaging property,” Estonian Prosecutor General Astrid Asi said in a statement issued by her office to Defense News.\nIn response to the sabotage attempt, the Estonian government will close Russian border crossings beginning on Dec. 1.\n“Any attempt to threaten Estonia’s security, sow fear, or act hostilely against us will be met with a decisive response,” Estonian Foreign Minister Margus Tsahkna was quoted in a statement released by his ministry. “Closing the border crossings will reduce Russia’s ability to conduct hostile activities against Estonia and help mitigate risks related to sanctions circumvention.”\nTsahkna said he would use a Nov. 16 meeting of the European Union’s foreign ministers in Brussels to discuss additional options aimed at Russia.\n“As long as Russia continues its war of aggression against Ukraine and threatens European security, we must increase pressure on all fronts,” he said. “This means tougher sanctions, a stricter visa policy, and additional measures to limit Russia’s ability to pursue its objectives.”\nThreod Systems produces a wide range of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), launcher systems for one-way drones, and electro-optical ISR and target acquisition systems. The Ukrainian military has successfully used the Estonian company’s drones and pneumatic launchers against invading Russian forces. This has put Threod Systems in Moscow’s crosshairs as Russia is scaling up its hybrid warfare attacks against European defense companies.\n“Whenever you see a long-range drone hit a target inside Russia, it is likely that our launcher was used by the Ukrainian military to facilitate that,” a Threod Systems representative told Defense News at the 2025 DSEI UK show in London.\nAlongside its deliveries to Ukraine, Threod Systems has also supplied its products to a number of militaries across the world, including in Europe, the Middle East and Africa. Establishing a presence in the U.S. market has also been one of its goals, according to company representatives.\nAs the manufacturer has ramped up its export sales, this has also been accompanied by investments to expand its production capacities and turnover growth. For 2025, Threod Systems reported revenues of some €47.2 million ($52 million), an increase of 24.1% compared with a year earlier, and a profit of €15.6 million, up 76.4% year-on-year, as indicated by figures from the company.\nThe latest sabotage is part of a series of arson attacks targeting Estonia’s defense industry. On Aug. 15, a fire broke out at the premises of Milrem Robotics , an Estonian unmanned ground vehicle producer, in Tallinn. The same month, three suspects were arrested in Latvia on suspicion of carrying out the attack, and are currently in custody in Estonia.\nIn a Sep. 29 statement, intelligence officials said the attack had been “commissioned by Russian security services,” citing similarities between the perpetrators’ modus operandi and those of other similar attacks carried out in Europe on Moscow’s behalf.\n“Defense industry companies, particularly those involved in drone technology, as well as organizations supporting Ukraine, have been among the targets of Russian security services since the beginning of Russia’s war of aggression against Ukraine,” KAPO head Palloson said.",
